    id: "testing-boundaries", category: "Testing", title: "Unit, integration, and end-to-end testing",
    summary: "Choose the smallest test that actually verifies the contract at risk.",
    what: "A unit test isolates a bounded behavior. An integration test exercises a meaningful collaboration or boundary such as real HTTP routing or relational persistence. An end-to-end test follows a complete user path through selected deployed components. The label matters less than exactly what evidence the test supplies.",
    why: "Mocked responses cannot prove database translation or middleware authorization, while an enormous browser suite is slow and hard to diagnose. Good test selection concentrates evidence at the boundary where a defect can actually occur.",
    how: "Start from an observable contract and failure mode. Keep fast business-rule tests focused, exercise persistence semantics with a suitable real provider, and reserve browser tests for interaction and navigation. Use controlled clocks and deterministic inputs, clean up owned data, and isolate parallel runs. Assert meaningful outcomes and error behavior rather than private helper call sequences that freeze implementation.",
