    summary: "Choose structures from operations and prove a window invariant before coding.",
    what: "Complexity describes how work or memory grows with input size under stated assumptions. A hash map trades space for lookup behavior; a sliding window maintains a contiguous range under an invariant. Neither is a universal replacement for understanding the exact problem.",
    why: "Recognizing the required operations avoids nested rescans. However, an average-case hash claim needs assumptions, and an expanding/shrinking window works only when its invariant can be maintained. For some sum problems, negative values invalidate the simple positive-number shrink rule.",
    how: "State constraints, a brute-force solution, and a correctness invariant. Choose data structures from lookup, order, update, and traversal needs. For the longest substring with no repeated character, track each character's latest position and only move the left boundary forward. Each input position is visited once, with extra storage for observed symbols. Define whether a character means UTF-16 code unit, Unicode scalar, or grapheme for the task.",
    flow: "Constraints -> baseline -> maintained invariant -> one-pass updates -> correctness -> time/space -> edge cases",
    example: "int LongestUnique(string s) {\n var last = new Dictionary<char, int>();\n int left = 0, best = 0;\n for (int right = 0; right < s.Length; right++) {\n  if (last.TryGetValue(s[right], out int p)) left = Math.Max(left, p + 1);\n  last[s[right]] = right;\n  best = Math.Max(best, right - left + 1);\n }\n return best;\n}\n// UTF-16 code-unit interpretation; expected O(n) time under normal hashing.",
